 "findings": [
  "Late Stage 2 means more risk in both halves of the 20 years: late-phase stocks fell into Stage 4 within 6 months more often (25% vs 17% in 2007–16, 29% vs 19% in 2017–26), and their median worst drop over the next 6 months was about −15% against −11% for early-phase stocks.",
  "Whether late also meant lower returns depended on the period. In 2017–26 late stocks beat NIFTY 500 by only +2.7% a year on average against +13.3% for early ones (median −8.3% vs −1.3%); in 2007–16 there was no return gap (+9.7% vs +9.5%). Read Late as 'tighten stops and size down', not 'sell everything'.",
  "The base count (IBD's rule) was the clearest single sign of maturity: +14.8% a year for stocks still in their first leg, +10.9% after one base, about +7.5% after two or three, and about +2% after four or more, with falls into Stage 4 rising from 19% to 30%.",
  "Time mattered too. The edge held for roughly the first 12 months in Stage 2, then dropped (12–24 months: about +4% a year, 28–30% fell into Stage 4) — close to Weinstein's 'nearly a year' for late Stage 2. Leaders still in Stage 2 after two years did better again (+8%), which is why time is only one of four parts of the phase.",
  "Distance above the 150-day line is not a warning: stocks 65–100% above it beat the index by +22.7% a year against +3.4% for those within 5% of it, and almost none fell into Stage 4 (the academic moving-average studies found the same). More than 100% above the 200-day line kept good average returns, but the worst 6-month drop deepened to about −17%, so it is shown as a climax warning rather than counted as lateness.",
  "The Stage 2 Score (strength) sorted outcomes in both halves: from about +4% a year under 40 to about +20% at 85+, and falls into Stage 4 from about 35% to 4–6%. Best combination: Early with a score of 70+ (+23.3% a year, 55% beat the index, 6% fell into Stage 4). Worst: Late with a score under 55 (+1.5%, 40% fell into Stage 4)."
 ],
 "caveats": "Only stocks still listed today (delisted companies are missing). Snapshots every 10 sessions overlap, so these are averages, not independent trades. Averages are trimmed at the 0.5% tails. Past results are evidence, not a promise.",
 "rules": "Stage 2 = close above the 150-day (30-week) average, which rose more than 1% in 20 sessions — the same as the Stage column elsewhere on the site. The advance starts at the first Stage 2 day after the last confirmed Stage 4 (10 of 20 sessions below a falling 150-day line). Phase points: base count since the start 0–4 (O'Neil: a base counts after a 20%+ gain from the last counted pivot; undercutting the previous base's low restarts at 1; two bases = 1 point, three = 2, four = 3, five+ = 4) · months in Stage 2 0–2 (6–12 months 1, 12+ months 2) · gain from the low before the start 0–2 (150–300% 1, over 300% 2) · 150-day slope slowing 0–1. Early 0–1, Mid 2–4, Late 5+. Stage 2 Score: RS 25 · 150-day slope 20 · close to the advance's peak 20 · above the 150-day 15 (30–100% best) · above the 50-day 10 · highest close in 6 years 10."
}